    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Event loop misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Event loop. The JavaScript event loop coordinates synchronous execution with queued asynchronous work so callbacks can run when the current stack is clear.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Event loop: The JavaScript event loop coordinates synchronous execution with queued asynchronous work so callbacks can run when the current stack is clear.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: JavaScript runs synchronous work on the call stack while queued asynchronous callbacks are processed by the event loop when the stack is clear.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Concurrency or distribution can improve throughput and resilience, but it introduces coordination, ordering, failure, and debugging concerns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nJavaScript runs synchronous work on the call stack while queued asynchronous callbacks are processed by the event loop when the stack is clear.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Event loop when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, javaScript runs synchronous work on the call stack while queued asynchronous callbacks are processed by the event loop when the stack is clear. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
