    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEvent propagation describes how DOM events travel through capture and bubbling phases between ancestors and the target.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Event propagation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Event propagation describes how DOM events travel through capture and bubbling phases between ancestors and the target.\n2. Use a concrete case: A browser event can travel through capture and bubble phases, so handlers and `stopPropagation()` affect which ancestors observe it.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Concurrency or distribution can improve throughput and resilience, but it introduces coordination, ordering, failure, and debugging concerns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Event propagation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA browser event can travel through capture and bubble phases, so handlers and `stopPropagation()` affect which ancestors observe it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Event propagation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a browser event can travel through capture and bubble phases, so handlers and `stopPropagation()` affect which ancestors observe it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Event propagation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
