    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about State.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of State comes from the behavior it provides: Frontend state is data that influences rendered UI and must have clear ownership, update rules, and lifecycle. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Keep state at the narrowest component level that needs it, and lift or centralize it only when multiple parts of the UI must share the same source of truth.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Frontend state is data that influences rendered UI and must have clear ownership, update rules, and lifecycle.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Keep state at the narrowest component level that needs it, and lift or centralize it only when multiple parts of the UI must share the same source of truth.\n3. State the trade-off: For State, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep state at the narrowest component level that needs it, and lift or centralize it only when multiple parts of the UI must share the same source of truth.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use State when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep state at the narrowest component level that needs it, and lift or centralize it only when multiple parts of the UI must share the same source of truth. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating State as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with State.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
