    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Routing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Routing. Frontend routing maps URLs or navigation actions to views, layouts, or application states.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Routing: Frontend routing maps URLs or navigation actions to views, layouts, or application states.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Routing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nClient-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Routing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Routing as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Routing.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
