    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Routing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRouting is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Routing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Routing gives you: Frontend routing maps URLs or navigation actions to views, layouts, or application states.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Routing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nClient-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Routing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Routing as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Routing.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
