    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Routing when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Routing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Frontend routing maps URLs or navigation actions to views, layouts, or application states.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Routing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nClient-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Routing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, client-side routing maps URLs to application views and should handle navigation, deep links, authorization, and loading/error states consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Routing as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Routing.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
