    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFrontend forms manage user input, validation, submission, and error feedback.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Forms accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Frontend forms manage user input, validation, submission, and error feedback.\n2. Use a concrete case: A form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Forms, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Forms first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Forms, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
