    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Forms misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Forms. Frontend forms manage user input, validation, submission, and error feedback.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Forms: Frontend forms manage user input, validation, submission, and error feedback.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Forms, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form should validate input, expose accessible labels and errors, and prevent duplicate submissions while the request is in flight. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
