    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Data fetching in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A frontend should handle loading, success, error, cancellation or stale responses, and caching according to the behavior users need.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Data fetching retrieves server or external data and must address loading, errors, caching, cancellation, and freshness.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A frontend should handle loading, success, error, cancellation or stale responses, and caching according to the behavior users need.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Data fetching, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA frontend should handle loading, success, error, cancellation or stale responses, and caching according to the behavior users need.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Data fetching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a frontend should handle loading, success, error, cancellation or stale responses, and caching according to the behavior users need. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data fetching as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data fetching.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
