    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you handle stale data and cache invalidation for Caching?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Caching.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
