    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Code splitting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCode splitting divides a JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that can be loaded when needed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Code splitting accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Code splitting divides a JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that can be loaded when needed.\n2. Use a concrete case: Split JavaScript bundles around routes or large features when it reduces initial load enough to justify the added loading complexity.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Code splitting, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Code splitting first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nSplit JavaScript bundles around routes or large features when it reduces initial load enough to justify the added loading complexity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Code splitting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, split JavaScript bundles around routes or large features when it reduces initial load enough to justify the added loading complexity. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Code splitting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Code splitting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Code splitting, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
