    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Accessibility.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Accessibility in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Accessibility makes interfaces usable across different abilities and assistive technologies through semantics, keyboard support, focus management, and appropriate contrast.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Accessibility, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
