    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Accessibility.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Accessibility when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Accessibility.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Accessibility makes interfaces usable across different abilities and assistive technologies through semantics, keyboard support, focus management, and appropriate contrast.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Accessibility, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Accessibility when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Accessibility.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Accessibility makes interfaces usable across different abilities and assistive technologies through semantics, keyboard support, focus management, and appropriate contrast.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Accessibility, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
