    slug: "frontend-engineer-what-is-security-161",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Frontend Engineer interview question about Security.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSecurity controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Security accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Security controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n2. Use a concrete case: A file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Security first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Security, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSecurity controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Security accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Security controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n2. Use a concrete case: A file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Security first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Security, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
