    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Security misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Security. Security controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Security: Security controls protect systems and data against unauthorized access, abuse, disruption, and disclosure.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA browser application may use Security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a file-upload endpoint can validate type and size, store files outside executable paths, enforce authorization, and log the security-relevant outcome. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
