    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSoftware testing evaluates whether software behaves as expected and reveals defects or risks before release. It provides evidence; it does not prove that a system has no defects.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Testing fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Software testing evaluates whether software behaves as expected and reveals defects or risks before release. It provides evidence; it does not prove that a system has no defects.\n2. Use a concrete case: A useful test plan starts from expected behavior, risk, and boundaries, then chooses the smallest set of checks that provides meaningful evidence.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Testing improves release confidence, but exhaustive testing is impossible, so scope should follow risk and requirements.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Testing fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA useful test plan starts from expected behavior, risk, and boundaries, then chooses the smallest set of checks that provides meaningful evidence.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Testing fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a useful test plan starts from expected behavior, risk, and boundaries, then chooses the smallest set of checks that provides meaningful evidence. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Testing fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
