    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTesting is used to find defects, validate requirements, expose risky behavior, and provide information for release decisions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Testing purpose accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Testing is used to find defects, validate requirements, expose risky behavior, and provide information for release decisions.\n2. Use a concrete case: Testing provides evidence about behavior and risk; it cannot prove that a non-trivial system contains no defects.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: More testing is not automatically better; poorly targeted cases add cost without reducing meaningful risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Testing purpose first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nTesting provides evidence about behavior and risk; it cannot prove that a non-trivial system contains no defects.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Testing purpose when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, testing provides evidence about behavior and risk; it cannot prove that a non-trivial system contains no defects. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Testing purpose, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
