    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define SDLC accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n2. Use a concrete case: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define SDLC first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor SDLC, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
