    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about SDLC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of SDLC comes from the behavior it provides: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n3. State the trade-off: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of SDLC comes from the behavior it provides: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n3. State the trade-off: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
