    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about SDLC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SDLC misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SDLC. The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SDLC: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SDLC misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SDLC. The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SDLC: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
