    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about SDLC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSDLC is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what SDLC gives you: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
