    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain SDLC in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: The software development life cycle describes the stages through which software is planned, built, tested, released, and maintained.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n4. Finish with the limitation: The exact process varies by organization; the important point is controlling work and feedback across the lifecycle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use SDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature can move through requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance with feedback between the stages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
