    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of STLC comes from the behavior it provides: The software testing life cycle organizes testing work such as requirement analysis, planning, test design, environment setup, execution, defect handling, and closure. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Testing work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: The software testing life cycle organizes testing work such as requirement analysis, planning, test design, environment setup, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Testing work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n3. State the trade-off: Formal stages help coordination but should not become paperwork that adds no useful evidence.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nTesting work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use STLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, testing work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating STLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with STLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
