    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about STLC when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to STLC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: The software testing life cycle organizes testing work such as requirement analysis, planning, test design, environment setup, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Testing work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Formal stages help coordination but should not become paperwork that adds no useful evidence.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nTesting work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use STLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, testing work can include requirement analysis, planning, design, environment preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating STLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with STLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
