    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Test cases.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Test cases accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n2. Use a concrete case: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Test cases first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test cases when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Test cases, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
