    slug: "qa-fresher-internship-how-does-test-cases-work-73",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Test cases.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Test cases is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test cases when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
