    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Test cases.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Test cases comes from the behavior it provides: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n3. State the trade-off: Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test cases when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
