    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Test cases misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Test cases. A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Test cases: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test cases when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
