    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTest cases is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Test cases gives you: A test case defines the setup, inputs, actions, and expected result for a specific check. Good cases make important behavior repeatable and reviewable.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Too many brittle implementation-level cases can increase maintenance cost; prioritize meaningful behavior.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test cases when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a password-reset test case can specify setup, input, action, expected result, and cleanup so another tester can reproduce it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
