    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Test scenarios reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Test scenarios.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A test scenario is a higher-level situation or behavior to evaluate; it can be decomposed into multiple detailed test cases.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: “User logs in with invalid credentials” is a scenario that can be split into cases for wrong passwords, unknown users, locked accounts, and rate limiting.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Scenarios provide coverage structure, while cases provide execution detail.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\n“User logs in with invalid credentials” is a scenario that can be split into cases for wrong passwords, unknown users, locked accounts, and rate limiting.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Test scenarios when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, “User logs in with invalid credentials” is a scenario that can be split into cases for wrong passwords, unknown users, locked accounts, and rate limiting. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
