    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA defect is a mismatch between actual behavior and expected behavior, based on requirements, specifications, or agreed product behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Defects accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A defect is a mismatch between actual behavior and expected behavior, based on requirements, specifications, or agreed product behavior.\n2. Use a concrete case: A useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Not every surprising behavior is a defect; expected behavior and product intent need to be established first.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Defects first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Defects when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defects as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defects.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Defects, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
