    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Defects when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Defects.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A defect is a mismatch between actual behavior and expected behavior, based on requirements, specifications, or agreed product behavior.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Not every surprising behavior is a defect; expected behavior and product intent need to be established first.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Defects when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a useful defect report includes the observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction steps, environment, and supporting evidence. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defects as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defects.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
