    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Severity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSeverity describes the technical or user impact of a defect. A severe issue can prevent a critical workflow or compromise data integrity or security.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Severity accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Severity describes the technical or user impact of a defect. A severe issue can prevent a critical workflow or compromise data integrity or security.\n2. Use a concrete case: A production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Severity should reflect impact, not the personal urgency of the reporter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Severity first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Severity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Severity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Severity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Severity, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
