    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSeverity is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Severity should reflect impact, not the personal urgency of the reporter. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Severity gives you: Severity describes the technical or user impact of a defect. A severe issue can prevent a critical workflow or compromise data integrity or security.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Severity should reflect impact, not the personal urgency of the reporter.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Severity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Severity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Severity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
