    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Severity when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Severity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Severity describes the technical or user impact of a defect. A severe issue can prevent a critical workflow or compromise data integrity or security.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Severity should reflect impact, not the personal urgency of the reporter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Severity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a production crash that blocks checkout has much higher severity than a small visual alignment issue. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Severity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Severity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
