    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Priority when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk. A concrete application is A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Priority provides the required behavior: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Priority can change with release context even when severity remains the same.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Priority fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Priority when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Priority as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Priority.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Priority when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk. A concrete application is A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Priority provides the required behavior: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Priority can change with release context even when severity remains the same.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Priority fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Priority when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Priority as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Priority.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
