    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Priority.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Priority misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Priority. Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Priority: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Priority can change with release context even when severity remains the same.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Priority when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Priority as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Priority.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
