    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Priority when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Priority.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Priority can change with release context even when severity remains the same.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Priority when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Priority as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Priority.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Priority when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Priority.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Priority describes how urgently a defect or task should be addressed relative to other work. It is a planning decision informed by impact, timing, and business risk.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Priority can change with release context even when severity remains the same.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Priority when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a release-critical defect can be prioritized ahead of a more severe issue in a rarely used internal feature because priority is a planning decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Priority as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Priority.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
