    question: "What is Functional testing?",
    slug: "qa-fresher-internship-what-is-functional-testing-121",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Functional testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFunctional testing checks whether a feature performs the behavior specified for it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Functional testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Functional testing checks whether a feature performs the behavior specified for it.\n2. Use a concrete case: An order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Functional coverage should include important negative and boundary behavior, not only successful requests.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Functional testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Functional testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Functional testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
