    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Functional testing are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Functional testing checks whether a feature performs the behavior specified for it.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: An order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Functional testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Functional testing. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
