    slug: "qa-fresher-internship-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-functional-testing-128",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Functional testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFunctional testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Functional coverage should include important negative and boundary behavior, not only successful requests. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Functional testing gives you: Functional testing checks whether a feature performs the behavior specified for it.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Functional coverage should include important negative and boundary behavior, not only successful requests.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Functional testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order API can be tested for creation, retrieval, cancellation, authorization, and validation failures against its documented behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
