    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSmoke is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Smoke tests should be small and high-value; a large suite defeats the purpose of a fast build-health signal. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Smoke gives you: Smoke testing is a focused check that a build or deployment is stable enough for deeper testing.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Smoke tests should be small and high-value; a large suite defeats the purpose of a fast build-health signal.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: After deployment, a smoke suite can check health, login, one critical API, and one core user flow before longer testing begins.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter deployment, a smoke suite can check health, login, one critical API, and one core user flow before longer testing begins.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Smoke when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after deployment, a smoke suite can check health, login, one critical API, and one core user flow before longer testing begins. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Smoke as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Smoke.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
