    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSanity testing is a focused check of the changed or affected area after a change, usually to confirm that the intended behavior works before broader testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Sanity accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Sanity testing is a focused check of the changed or affected area after a change, usually to confirm that the intended behavior works before broader testing.\n2. Use a concrete case: After changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The exact use of the term varies by team, so describe the scope rather than relying on the label alone.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Sanity first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Sanity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sanity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sanity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Sanity, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
