    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Sanity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Sanity when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Sanity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Sanity testing is a focused check of the changed or affected area after a change, usually to confirm that the intended behavior works before broader testing.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: After changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: The exact use of the term varies by team, so describe the scope rather than relying on the label alone.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Sanity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sanity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sanity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Sanity when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Sanity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Sanity testing is a focused check of the changed or affected area after a change, usually to confirm that the intended behavior works before broader testing.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: After changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: The exact use of the term varies by team, so describe the scope rather than relying on the label alone.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Sanity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after changing coupon calculation, first verify the coupon-related paths before spending the entire regression budget. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sanity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sanity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    explanation: "What advanced follow-up question could be asked about Sanity? is a core QA Fresher-internship interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
