    slug: "qa-fresher-internship-what-is-regression-151",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Regression.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRegression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Regression accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n2. Use a concrete case: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Regression first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Regression, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
