    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Regression addresses it: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Regression addresses it: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
    explanation: "Why is Regression important? is a core QA Fresher-internship interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
