    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Regression.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Regression comes from the behavior it provides: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n3. State the trade-off: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
