    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Regression.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Regression misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Regression. Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Regression: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
