    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Regression in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Regression testing checks that existing behavior still works after a change.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Regression scope should be risk-based; rerunning every test for every change may be inefficient.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
