    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRetesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Retesting accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n2. Use a concrete case: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Retesting first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Retesting, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
