    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Retesting is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
