    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Retesting comes from the behavior it provides: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n3. State the trade-off: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
