    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Retesting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Retesting when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix. A concrete application is After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Retesting provides the required behavior: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Retesting fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
