    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Retesting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Retesting misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Retesting. Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Retesting: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Retesting misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Retesting. Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Retesting: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    explanation: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving Retesting? is a core QA Fresher-internship interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
