    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Retesting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRetesting is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Retesting gives you: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
