    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Retesting reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Retesting.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Retesting verifies that a previously failing defect now behaves correctly after a fix.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: After fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Retesting proves the specific fix; regression testing checks for unintended side effects elsewhere.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Retesting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after fixing an expired-order cancellation defect, rerun the original failing case to verify the fix before widening the regression scope. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
