    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Positive testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPositive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Positive testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n2. Use a concrete case: Submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Positive cases alone can miss validation and failure behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Positive testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nSubmit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Positive testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Positive testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
