    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Positive testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Positive testing. Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Positive testing: Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Positive cases alone can miss validation and failure behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nSubmit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Positive testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Positive testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Positive testing. Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Positive testing: Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Positive cases alone can miss validation and failure behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nSubmit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Positive testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
