    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA Fresher-internship interview question about Positive testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPositive testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Positive cases alone can miss validation and failure behavior. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Positive testing gives you: Positive testing uses valid inputs and expected usage paths to confirm that supported behavior works.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Positive cases alone can miss validation and failure behavior.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nSubmit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Positive testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, submit valid credentials and expected input formats to confirm supported behavior works. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
