    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNegative testing deliberately uses invalid, unexpected, or unsupported inputs to verify that the system fails safely and predictably.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Negative testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Negative testing deliberately uses invalid, unexpected, or unsupported inputs to verify that the system fails safely and predictably.\n2. Use a concrete case: Send malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Negative testing should reflect realistic abuse and boundary conditions rather than random invalid data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Negative testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nSend malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Negative testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, send malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Negative testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
