    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Negative testing comes from the behavior it provides: Negative testing deliberately uses invalid, unexpected, or unsupported inputs to verify that the system fails safely and predictably. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Send malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Negative testing deliberately uses invalid, unexpected, or unsupported inputs to verify that the system fails safely and predictably.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Send malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state.\n3. State the trade-off: Negative testing should reflect realistic abuse and boundary conditions rather than random invalid data.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nSend malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software test cycle may use Negative testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, send malformed or unauthorized requests and verify the system returns the intended error without corrupting state. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
