    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Test planning are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Test planning defines scope, approach, resources, environments, data, timing, dependencies, and exit criteria for testing.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: For a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Test planning. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
