    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Test planning misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Test planning. Test planning defines scope, approach, resources, environments, data, timing, dependencies, and exit criteria for testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Test planning: Test planning defines scope, approach, resources, environments, data, timing, dependencies, and exit criteria for testing.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: For a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A detailed plan that ignores risk can waste effort; plans should stay aligned with change impact.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
