    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Test planning in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: For a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Test planning defines scope, approach, resources, environments, data, timing, dependencies, and exit criteria for testing.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: For a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n4. Finish with the limitation: A detailed plan that ignores risk can waste effort; plans should stay aligned with change impact.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a payment release, identify affected platforms, test data, dependencies, high-risk paths, environments, and exit criteria before execution starts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
