    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA test strategy explains the overall approach to achieving sufficient coverage and release confidence, including test levels, automation, environments, and risk focus.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Test strategy accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A test strategy explains the overall approach to achieving sufficient coverage and release confidence, including test levels, automation, environments, and risk focus.\n2. Use a concrete case: A service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Strategy should match architecture and risk; there is no universal percentage of automation that guarantees quality.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Test strategy first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Test strategy, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
