    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Test strategy.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Test strategy misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Test strategy. A test strategy explains the overall approach to achieving sufficient coverage and release confidence, including test levels, automation, environments, and risk focus.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Test strategy: A test strategy explains the overall approach to achieving sufficient coverage and release confidence, including test levels, automation, environments, and risk focus.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Strategy should match architecture and risk; there is no universal percentage of automation that guarantees quality.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
