    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Test strategy in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: A test strategy explains the overall approach to achieving sufficient coverage and release confidence, including test levels, automation, environments, and risk focus.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Strategy should match architecture and risk; there is no universal percentage of automation that guarantees quality.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Test strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service may combine unit tests in CI, API integration tests for contracts, and a smaller UI suite for critical journeys instead of treating every test level equally. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
