    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Risk based testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Risk-based testing prioritizes test effort according to likelihood and impact of failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Risk-based testing prioritizes test effort according to likelihood and impact of failure.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Risk ranking is an informed judgment, so document assumptions and revisit them when the product changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Risk based testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
