    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRisk based testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Risk ranking is an informed judgment, so document assumptions and revisit them when the product changes. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Risk based testing gives you: Risk-based testing prioritizes test effort according to likelihood and impact of failure.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Risk ranking is an informed judgment, so document assumptions and revisit them when the product changes.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Risk based testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
