    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Risk based testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Risk based testing reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Risk based testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Risk-based testing prioritizes test effort according to likelihood and impact of failure.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Risk ranking is an informed judgment, so document assumptions and revisit them when the product changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Risk based testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment authorization path deserves deeper testing than a cosmetic settings page because failure has a larger customer and financial impact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
