    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Estimation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTest estimation predicts the effort and time needed for planned testing based on scope, complexity, environments, data, dependencies, and team capacity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Estimation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Test estimation predicts the effort and time needed for planned testing based on scope, complexity, environments, data, dependencies, and team capacity.\n2. Use a concrete case: Estimate a browser-coverage matrix separately from API execution because each browser/device combination adds effort.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Estimates are uncertain; state assumptions and update them as unknowns are resolved.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Estimation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nEstimate a browser-coverage matrix separately from API execution because each browser/device combination adds effort.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Estimation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, estimate a browser-coverage matrix separately from API execution because each browser/device combination adds effort. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Estimation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Estimation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Estimation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
