    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Defect lifecycle when the problem matches the behavior it provides: The defect lifecycle tracks a defect from discovery through triage, assignment, fixing, retesting, and closure or rejection. A concrete application is A failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Defect lifecycle provides the required behavior: The defect lifecycle tracks a defect from discovery through triage, assignment, fixing, retesting, and closure or rejection.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Teams may use different status names; the important point is clear ownership and evidence at each transition.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Defect lifecycle fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Defect lifecycle when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect lifecycle as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect lifecycle.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
