    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDefect lifecycle is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Teams may use different status names; the important point is clear ownership and evidence at each transition. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Defect lifecycle gives you: The defect lifecycle tracks a defect from discovery through triage, assignment, fixing, retesting, and closure or rejection.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Teams may use different status names; the important point is clear ownership and evidence at each transition.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Defect lifecycle when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a failed checkout test is logged, triaged, assigned, fixed, retested, and closed only after the expected behavior is confirmed. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect lifecycle as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect lifecycle.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
