    slug: "qa-1-2-years-of-expirance-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-triage-57",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Triage.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Triage misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Triage. Defect triage evaluates severity, priority, reproducibility, scope, and ownership to decide what should happen next.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Triage: Defect triage evaluates severity, priority, reproducibility, scope, and ownership to decide what should happen next.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: During release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Triage should be evidence-based and time-bounded rather than becoming a debate over labels.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nDuring release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Triage when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, during release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Triage as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Triage.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
