    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Triage when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Triage.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Defect triage evaluates severity, priority, reproducibility, scope, and ownership to decide what should happen next.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: During release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Triage should be evidence-based and time-bounded rather than becoming a debate over labels.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nDuring release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Triage when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, during release triage, compare a data-corruption defect with a cosmetic issue and prioritize according to customer and business impact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Triage as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Triage.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
