    slug: "qa-1-2-years-of-expirance-what-is-traceability-61",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Traceability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTraceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Traceability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Traceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified.\n2. Use a concrete case: Map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Traceability is useful for coverage and audits, but unnecessary links can become maintenance overhead.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Traceability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nMap the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Traceability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Traceability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Traceability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Traceability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
