    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Traceability is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Traceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Traceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Traceability is useful for coverage and audits, but unnecessary links can become maintenance overhead.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nMap the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Traceability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Traceability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Traceability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
