    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Traceability comes from the behavior it provides: Traceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Traceability links requirements to tests and, where useful, defects or evidence so teams can see what has and has not been verified.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n3. State the trade-off: Traceability is useful for coverage and audits, but unnecessary links can become maintenance overhead.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nMap the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Traceability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, map the requirement “only account owners may export data” to authorization tests and their results. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Traceability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Traceability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
