    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Exploratory testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Exploratory testing combines learning, test design, and execution while following observations rather than only a prewritten script.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Exploratory testing combines learning, test design, and execution while following observations rather than only a prewritten script.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: While testing a file-upload feature, vary file sizes, names, network interruptions, duplicate uploads, and navigation to discover unexpected behavior.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Exploratory testing is powerful for discovery but still benefits from notes, charters, and clear boundaries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nWhile testing a file-upload feature, vary file sizes, names, network interruptions, duplicate uploads, and navigation to discover unexpected behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Exploratory testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, while testing a file-upload feature, vary file sizes, names, network interruptions, duplicate uploads, and navigation to discover unexpected behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
