    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about State transitions when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to State transitions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: State-transition testing verifies behavior as an object moves between valid and invalid states.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: For an order, test new->paid, paid->shipped, shipped->delivered, and invalid transitions such as delivered->paid.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: The strongest tests include transition guards and unexpected events, not just happy-path states.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an order, test new->paid, paid->shipped, shipped->delivered, and invalid transitions such as delivered->paid.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use State transitions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for an order, test new->paid, paid->shipped, shipped->delivered, and invalid transitions such as delivered->paid. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating State transitions as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with State transitions.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
