    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPostman is an API client and collaboration tool commonly used to send requests, inspect responses, organize collections, and run API checks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Postman accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Postman is an API client and collaboration tool commonly used to send requests, inspect responses, organize collections, and run API checks.\n2. Use a concrete case: Create a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: It is useful for exploratory and automated API checks, but it is not a substitute for the complete CI test strategy.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Postman first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Postman when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, create a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Postman as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Postman.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Postman, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
