    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Postman comes from the behavior it provides: Postman is an API client and collaboration tool commonly used to send requests, inspect responses, organize collections, and run API checks. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Create a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Postman is an API client and collaboration tool commonly used to send requests, inspect responses, organize collections, and run API checks.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Create a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields.\n3. State the trade-off: It is useful for exploratory and automated API checks, but it is not a substitute for the complete CI test strategy.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Postman when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, create a collection for `/orders`, add environment variables for the base URL and token, and use assertions for status and response fields. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Postman as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Postman.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
